Kane-Mele on the honeycomb, PRL 95, 226801 (2005): four bands, two occupied,
a staggered sublattice potential, spin-orbit coupling and a Rashba term.

1. delta = 1.0, rashba = 0.25
   realized group   : #156.50 P3m11', orbitals at Wyckoff 1c and 1b, 4 bands
                      (measured from the Hamiltonian: the staggered potential
                      puts the two honeycomb sites on inequivalent positions)
   valence content  : GM: -GM6   K: -K5 + -K6   M: -M3M4
      -> 1 x -E1↑G(2)@1a(3m1',3m), a position no orbital sits on
   conduction       : GM: -GM6   K: 2×-K4   M: -M3M4
      -> none; as a difference −[-E1↑G(2)@1a] +[-E1↑G(2)@1b] +[-E1↑G(2)@1c]
   indicator group  : X_BS(#156.50) = trivial, so these indicators do not distinguish
                      the valence pair from an atomic limit at 1a

2. delta = 2.5, rashba = 0.25
   realized group   : #156.50 P3m11', orbitals at Wyckoff 1c and 1b
   valence content  : GM: -GM6   K: -K4 + -K5   M: -M3M4
      -> 1 x -E1↑G(2)@1b(3m1',3m), a site an orbital sits on
   conduction       : GM: -GM6   K: -K4 + -K6   M: -M3M4
      -> 1 x -E1↑G(2)@1c(3m1',3m)
   the group is #156.50 in both regimes and the valence pair's position
   moves from 1a to 1b with the regime

3. delta = 0, rashba = 0
   realized group   : #183.186 P6mm1', orbitals at Wyckoff 2b
                      (delta = 0 puts the two honeycomb sites on one orbit, and
                      the group is the one the staggered potential lowered to #156.50)
   valence content  : GM: -GM9   K: -K6   M: -M5
      -> 1 x -E1↑G(2)@1a(6mm1',6mm), a position no orbital sits on
   conduction       : GM: -GM8   K: -K4 + -K5   M: -M5
      -> none; as a difference −[-E1↑G(2)@1a] +[-E1↑G(4)@2b]

4. delta = 0, rashba = 0.25
   realized group   : #183.186 P6mm1', orbitals at Wyckoff 2b
   valence content  : GM: -GM9   K: -K6   M: -M5
      -> 1 x -E1↑G(2)@1a(6mm1',6mm)
   conduction       : GM: -GM8   K: -K4 + -K5   M: -M5
      -> none; as a difference −[-E1↑G(2)@1a] +[-E1↑G(4)@2b]
   K level by level : -K6 @ -1.558846 (dim 2)   -K4 @ +0.808846 (dim 1)
                      -K5 @ +2.308846 (dim 1)

5. the conserved operator, read off H(k) on a 6x6 mesh of in-plane k
   delta 0, rashba 0     : the commutant of H(k) is the identity and 1 more
                           operator, and s_z commutes with H(k) at every k,
                           max |[s_z, H(k)]| = 0.000000: that operator is s_z
   delta 0, rashba 0.25  : the commutant is the identity and 0 more,
                           the Rashba term mixing the spins: max |[s_z, H(k)]| = 1.500000
   delta 1.0, rashba 0.25: the commutant is the identity and 0 more

6. what the lost operator does to the degeneracies
   the group is #183.186 at both rashba values and the content above is the same
   entry for entry; what differs is the commutant
   multiplets       : GM (2, 2) -> (2, 2)   K (2, 2) -> (2, 1, 1)   M (2, 2) -> (2, 2)
                      at rashba = 0 and at rashba = 0.25
   the two levels K splits into, over the operations (E, C3(z), C3^-1(z), m([2-10]), m(y), m([110])):
      -K4 @ +0.808846 : (+1, -1, -1, -1i, -1i, -1i)
      -K5 @ +2.308846 : (+1, -1, -1, +1i, +1i, +1i)
      one row is the conjugate of the other: two distinct one-dimensional
      coreps, so the little group holds neither of them to the other, and what
      held them together at rashba = 0 was s_z
      (BCS's labels attach to -k, so an operation taking k to -k interchanges
      the two names; the characters are the observable)
   -K6 is a corep of dimension 2 and stays one level whatever the commutant does
   the splitting    : rashba 0.05 -> 0.300000, 0.125 -> 0.750000, 0.25 -> 1.500000
                      split / rashba = 6.000000 / 6.000000 / 6.000000

7. the deep atomic limit: delta = 50
   the valence pair carries 99.96 % of its weight on the orbital at Wyckoff 1b,
   0.04 % on the one at 1c
   decompose        : 1 x -E1↑G(2)@1b(3m1',3m) — the site the electrons are on

8. the bands along GM-K-M-GM, one panel per regime

